dict(id='E1',kind='Exhibition',title='Shared Sea Atlas',site='Awashima / a maritime history setting',season='Autumn 2028',concept='A wall of paired maps connects the Seto Inland Sea to the larger systems that pass through it. Each world map has a Japan companion. Visitors move between the two scales, then leave a question on a paper table.',reason='Awashima’s shipping history gives the work a local starting point. The Maritime Memorial Hall or another agreed indoor room is a candidate, not a booked venue. The work asks residents which routes and materials deserve attention.',experience=['Start with Earth and Water. Find Japan without placing it at the centre of the world.','Compare six world/Japan pairs: land, water, cables, atmosphere, refinery throughput and temperature.','At a seated table, add an anonymous question or draw a route that matters to island life. Visitors may take their contribution home.'],works=['earth','water','network-fiber','cloud-atmosphere','resources-energy-petrochemical-refinery-throughput-latest-2024','anthropocene-temperature-2026'],footprint='One indoor room, approximately 6 × 8 m; at least 8 m of usable wall; six paired panels and one 1.8 × 0.8 m table.',materials='Twelve pigment prints, each 1,117.6 × 483.9 mm; reusable hanging rails; printed Japanese/English labels; paper and pencils. No network connection is needed.',budget=[('Printing and mounting',420000),('Reusable display and table',220000),('Artist preparation and local co-design',450000),('Translation and accessible labels',180000),('Packing, freight and local travel',210000),('Installation and removal labour',180000),('Travel and accommodation allowance',180000),('Contingency',210000)],review='Confirm the room, wall loads, clear routes and host capacity. Ask island partners to select the local questions and agree how contributions will be kept or returned.',image='water'),
dict(id='E2',kind='Exhibition',title='The Port and the Cloud',site='Uno or Takamatsu / an indoor port-side room',season='Any 2028 season',concept='Four map pairs show the physical systems behind a familiar word: cloud. Cables, data centres, ground stations and atmospheric clouds sit beside one another. A reading table makes their different meanings clear.',reason='A port already connects visible movement with distant infrastructure. The display uses that experience without suggesting that a cable line proves a local landing or that a data-centre point measures electricity use.',experience=['Follow one cable route across the world plate.','Compare it with the groundstation and cloud/CDN inventories, keeping their dates visible.','Read the atmosphere plate as weather observations with distinct acquisition dates. Write one question about what remains unseen.'],works=['network-fiber','network-cdn','network-groundstations','cloud-atmosphere'],footprint='Indoor room around 5 × 6 m; four paired wall panels; one low reading table; seated alternative to every wall text.',materials='Eight full-size prints; freestanding reusable panels; a source booklet; paper question cards. Optional recorded conversation only after speaker agreement.',budget=[('Printing and panels',380000),('Artist research and editing',420000),('Local host and participation fees',180000),('Translation and accessible materials',150000),('Freight and installation',220000),('Travel and accommodation',180000),('Contingency',170000)],review='Confirm a host room outside ferry circulation. Review infrastructure captions for unsupported claims. Obtain speaker and recording agreements before producing audio.',image='network-fiber'),
dict(id='E3',kind='Exhibition',title='Heat, Salt, Water',site='Utazu / near the town’s salt-making history',season='Autumn 2028',concept='A small atlas puts land temperature, water, food, fisheries and oil refining beside one another. The installation invites comparison of systems while making their different units and time periods visible.',reason='Utazu’s salt-making history offers a concrete entry into energy, evaporation and labour. Residents can correct the exhibition’s questions before the final labels are printed.',experience=['Begin with the water plate and a short account of the host’s chosen local history.','Read the temperature map as record-day counts, not as a forecast of island weather.','Compare food and fisheries statistics with refinery throughput. A caption explains what each measure can and cannot say about the islands.'],works=['water','anthropocene-temperature-2026','resources-food-food-production-index-latest-2024','resources-fauna-fisheries-production-latest-2024','resources-energy-petrochemical-refinery-throughput-latest-2024'],footprint='Indoor room around 6 × 6 m; five paired panels; one reading table; no process equipment or water installation.',materials='Ten full-size prints; modular rails; source cards; tactile sample of folded paper. No extraction, salt production demonstration or environmental sensing is proposed.',budget=[('Prints and mounting',400000),('Artist development',450000),('Local consultation and host fees',220000),('Translation and access',170000),('Installation and freight',260000),('Travel and accommodation',220000),('Contingency',230000)],review='Agree the local historical account and credit contributors. Have a reader check that national statistics are not presented as measurements of Utazu.',image='anthropocene-temperature-2026'),
dict(id='V1',kind='Event',title='Fold a World, Read an Island',site='Shido or Takamatsu / an accessible workshop room',season='Spring 2028',concept='Participants cut, fold and annotate a paper world map. A world/Japan comparison becomes a practical lesson in how a map changes attention. Each person chooses whether to keep or share their sheet.',reason='The supplied conference ideas include a rip, burn, fold workshop. This proposal develops the paper-folding part into a low-equipment public session; it uses no burning.',experience=['A ten-minute demonstration compares globe, flat map and Japan detail.','In pairs, fold an A3 study sheet and mark a route, material or question.','A closing table conversation lets participants explain their choices; no personal location data is collected.'],works=['geometry','graticules','earth','water'],footprint='Two 90-minute sessions, up to 20 people each; a 6 × 6 m accessible room; five worktables and a seated demonstration.',materials='A3 study prints, blunt scissors, pencils, reusable templates and large-print instructions. Full-size masters remain available as wall references.',budget=[('Facilitation and preparation',160000),('Local co-facilitator',80000),('Printing and materials',60000),('Translation and access',60000),('Room and local transport allowance',40000),('Contingency',50000)],review='Confirm the host, participant age range and access needs. Review translations and provide a no-cut alternative. Photography requires separate participant agreement.',image='geometry'),
dict(id='V2',kind='Event',title='What Does the Cloud Touch?',site='Takamatsu / a public conversation room',season='Any 2028 season',concept='A moderated conversation connects digital networks to cables, ports, energy and everyday work. Three map pairs anchor the discussion. The audience leaves with a short source guide.',reason='The conference memo names cloud/network and media themes. Nicole Starosielski, Jennifer Holt, Lisa Nakamura and Eric Rodenbeck are possible invitation leads, not confirmed contributors.',experience=['A short map reading establishes what the sources measure.','Two invited contributors and a local respondent discuss one concrete route or material dependency.','Audience questions are followed by a facilitated source-table conversation, with an option to contribute in writing.'],works=['network-fiber','network-cdn','petroleum-eia-liquids-2025'],footprint='One 90-minute conversation for about 50 visitors; accessible indoor seating; three paired maps and a source table.',materials='Six reference prints, microphones if the room requires them, bilingual handout and live interpretation allowance. No recording is assumed.',budget=[('Speaker and moderator fees',240000),('Local respondent and preparation',80000),('Interpretation and access',180000),('Room and technical allowance',70000),('Printing',40000),('Travel allowance',90000),('Contingency',80000)],review='Secure contributors, define a topic with the local respondent, and confirm interpretation. International travel beyond the allowance would require a revised budget or remote participation.',image='network-cdn'),
dict(id='V3',kind='Event',title='An Island Seen from Two Scales',site='Megijima / a host-selected accessible route',season='Spring or autumn 2028',concept='A short guided walk alternates between looking at the island and reading maps. Participants record observations in words or sketches. The activity ends at a seated table so walking is optional.',reason='Megijima’s shore, wind walls and settlement offer places to discuss what a global map leaves out. A resident host selects the route and the subjects; this is not a survey of residents.',experience=['Meet indoors and choose a walking or seated version.','Visit three agreed stopping places over 45 minutes. Compare observed surroundings with dated map layers.','Return for a 30-minute table discussion. Keep sketches with their makers unless they explicitly choose to contribute them.'],works=['earth','water','cloud-atmosphere'],footprint='Two sessions of up to 12 people; route under 1 km if approved; an indoor alternative for weather or access.',materials='A4 study sheets, clipboards, pencils and a large seated map. No tracking app, drone or collection of identifiable location histories.',budget=[('Resident guide and planning',100000),('Facilitation',80000),('Translation and access',60000),('Printing and materials',30000),('Local transport and room allowance',40000),('Contingency',40000)],review='Walk the route with the host; confirm step-free alternatives, tide/weather decisions and a cancellation contact. Keep visitor routes clear of working areas.',image='earth'),
dict(id='I1',kind='Site installation',title='The Port Reading Table',site='Uno / an agreed indoor waiting or community space',season='Any 2028 season',concept='A durable table holds a sequence of world and Japan map sheets. A traveller can read one sheet in a minute or stay with the whole atlas. The table operates without a screen or network.',reason='The format meets the variable time of a port visit. It must sit in a host-controlled room with free circulation, not in a boarding queue or an emergency route.',experience=['Choose a tab: water, cables, energy or heat.','Turn a world sheet to its Japan companion.','Read the date and one clear limitation; take a small printed guide if wanted.'],works=['water','network-fiber','petroleum-eia-liquids-2025','anthropocene-temperature-2026'],footprint='About 3 × 3 m including circulation; one 1.8 × 0.8 m table with seated reach and knee clearance; eight A2 study sheets.',materials='Reusable timber or metal table, replaceable protected paper sheets, tabbed bindings and printed guide. Full print masters supply the source artwork.',budget=[('Table fabrication',320000),('Prints and replaceable covers',180000),('Artist preparation',220000),('Host consultation and access review',100000),('Translation',90000),('Freight, install and removal',160000),('Contingency',130000)],review='Confirm dimensions with access users and host staff; approve cleaning, sheet replacement and storage. Test glare and reading distance on a physical sample.',image='water'),
dict(id='I2',kind='Site installation',title='Maps in the Wind’s Shelter',site='Ogijima / a sheltered, non-heritage courtyard candidate',season='One 2028 season, subject to exposure review',concept='Three reversible fabric panels place world maps on one face and Japan details on the other. Visitors turn a panel by hand within a sheltered structure. The work makes scale a physical choice.',reason='Ogijima’s dense settlement and wind-protection practices inform the idea. The structure must be designed for the actual site and must not attach to historic walls.',experience=['Approach three panels at walking speed.','Turn one panel to compare world and Japan views.','Read a short seated copy of each label and return the panel to its resting position.'],works=['water','network-fiber','anthropocene-temperature-2026'],footprint='Indicative 4 × 4 m footprint; three 1.2 × 0.65 m panels; maximum proposed height 1.8 m. Dimensions are design assumptions pending site and engineering review.',materials='Printed fabric, rounded reusable frames, controlled hinges and an engineered support. No ground anchoring or historic-wall fixing is assumed.',budget=[('Site-specific structural design',250000),('Fabrication and hardware',650000),('Printed panels and spares',250000),('Artist development',350000),('Local consultation, translation and access',220000),('Freight, install and removal',300000),('Contingency',330000)],review='Required before fabrication: site permission, structural review, wind and weather operating limits, pinch-point review and an indoor fallback. This is the most complex option.',image='network-fiber'),
dict(id='I3',kind='Site installation',title='The Island Atlas Desk',site='Ibukijima / a former-school or community-room candidate',season='Autumn 2028',concept='An ordinary desk becomes a compact atlas of sea, food, energy and sky. Fold-out sheets and a source book let visitors read at their own pace. A host can change the sequence during the season.',reason='The supplied site report connects Ibukijima with iriko production and a former school. A desk respects the scale of a room and leaves room for a locally chosen account of work and sea.',experience=['Open a labelled drawer or folder.','Unfold one world sheet beside its Japan companion.','Read a short local text commissioned with the host, then return the sheet for the next visitor.'],works=['water','resources-fauna-fisheries-production-latest-2024','resources-energy-petrochemical-refinery-throughput-latest-2024','astro-observer-ground-multiband'],footprint='About 3 × 3 m; one adapted 1.6 × 0.8 m desk, two chairs and a wall reference. No power or internet is needed.',materials='Repairable desk, four pairs of folded A2 sheets, large-print source book and replaceable folders. Celestial sheets are clearly labelled as Setouchi observer views.',budget=[('Desk adaptation',180000),('Prints, folds and source book',180000),('Artist preparation',220000),('Local text commission',100000),('Translation and access',90000),('Freight and installation',100000),('Contingency',110000)],review='Agree room use, desk access and local text ownership. Test folding durability and give the host spare copies and a clear replacement method.',image='resources-fauna-fisheries-production-latest-2024')
